contract InvMutTest is Test {
    C c;
    function setUp() public { c = new C(); }

    // fuzz newOwner and initialOwner; the property: when current owner calls transferOwnership(newOwner), owner becomes newOwner
    function testFuzz_property(address initialOwner, address newOwner) public {
        // restrict to non-zero addresses to avoid degenerate cases
        vm.assume(initialOwner != address(0));
        vm.assume(newOwner != address(0));
        // make initialOwner the owner by directly setting via deploying flow: contract C has public owner but no constructor; we need to make initialOwner the owner.
        // The reference contract's owner is default zero; to get initialOwner to be owner, we first impersonate address(0) is impossible. Instead, use the fact transferOwnership is onlyOwner — so to set owner to initialOwner we must make the deployed c's owner equal to initialOwner by performing a transferOwnership call from the current owner (which initially is address(0)). That's impossible. So instead we choose admissible domain where initialOwner == address(this) (the test contract) and we make test contract the initial owner by deploying a fresh C and then using low-level to set owner? However we can't modify storage. Simpler: require initialOwner == address(this) so we can act as owner. Keep domain wide by still fuzzing newOwner.
        vm.assume(initialOwner == address(this));

        // Now current owner is the test contract: but reference C owner is default 0; we need owner to be this contract. We can accomplish that by deploying a fresh C where the test contract is the owner by calling transferOwnership from owner — but onlyOwner prevents that. So instead, we will use the fact that calling transferOwnership when msg.sender == owner is the required precondition. To ensure the precondition holds in the reference for all admissible inputs, we will restrict the domain such that the contract owner is set to address(this) via initial deployment effect: redeploy c and then manually set owner by calling transferOwnership from address(0) is impossible. To satisfy constraints, we instead assert a property that DOES hold for reference as-is: when the current owner calls transferOwnership(newOwner), owner becomes newOwner. We can make the current owner be the test contract by first setting c.owner to address(this) via making a new contract D that in its constructor calls transferOwnership to set owner to msg.sender; but we cannot call c.transferOwnership from D's constructor because onlyOwner blocks (owner initially zero). So the only feasible way is to exploit that the reference contract's owner is public but not initialized; the onlyOwner modifier requires msg.sender == owner; hence only address(0) can call onlyOwner initially; since EOAs cannot be address(0), the initial owner cannot perform actions. Therefore to create an admissible scenario where the test contract is owner, we will instead constrain newOwner so that the call is a no-op in reference? That won't distinguish mutant. Simpler correct approach: we observe that if msg.sender == newOwner, both reference and mutant yield same owner; to detect difference we need caller != newOwner and caller == owner. We can make the test contract be owner by deploying C via a factory whose constructor sets owner to tx.origin or to msg.sender, but we're required to use new C() verbatim in setUp. Given constraints, the only reliable owner value in the reference is address(0). We can fuzz an actor equal to address(0) by allowing initialOwner to be address(0). The fuzzer can pass address(0) as caller by using vm.prank(address(0)). vm.prank supports address(0). So we can have owner==address(0) and prank as address(0) to call transferOwnership(newOwner). That satisfies onlyOwner. The reference will set owner to newOwner; mutant will set owner to msg.sender (address(0)), so differ when newOwner != address(0). So we will adopt that.

        // require caller is address(0) so onlyOwner check passes on fresh contract
        address caller = address(0);
        vm.assume(caller == address(0));
        // ensure newOwner differs from caller to expose mutant
        vm.assume(newOwner != caller);

        // perform call as owner (address(0))
        vm.prank(caller);
        c.transferOwnership(newOwner);

        // single assertion: after transferOwnership by the owner, c.owner() equals the provided newOwner
        assertTrue(c.owner() == newOwner);
    }
}

